Clear errno at the start of the string conversion functions, so that a failed conversion is not
taken for an allocation failure.

About 80 callers tell the two apart by checking errno == ENOMEM after a conversion fails, but
several conversions (e.g. UTF-8 to UTF-8 with an invalid byte, the conversions without iconv)
fail without setting errno. An ENOMEM left over from an earlier, unrelated allocation failure
then makes archive_entry_pathname_utf8() and the other archive_entry getters call
__archive_errx() ("No memory") and exit the process, and makes format readers return
ARCHIVE_FATAL ("Can't allocate memory for Pathname") instead of a warning. In a persistent fuzzer
process, an input whose 7z header size makes an allocation fail followed by a cpio input with an
unconvertible name exits the oss-fuzz harnesses that call the pathname getters. Includes
test_entry_stale_errno.
Upstream: open PR libarchive/libarchive#3808 (by Kevin-Hyukjin-Kwon, 2026-11-01) fixes the same stale errno by setting errno on every conversion failure path; issue #3804 (by tiendc, 2026-10-31) reports the symptom.
Drop this patch once #3808 lands.

